    <string name="rules_title">Rules</string>
    <string name="welcome_title">Peg Solitaire</string>
    <string name="welcome_sub">One player. Hop pegs until one is left.</string>
    <string name="rules_1">The middle hole starts empty.</string>
    <string name="rules_2">Hop a peg over its neighbour into an empty hole. Straight lines only, no diagonals.</string>
    <string name="rules_3">The peg you hopped over comes out and goes to the tray.</string>
    <string name="rules_4">Keep going until you\'re stuck. Fewer left is better. Last peg dead centre is a full solve.</string>
    <string name="rules_mistakes">Easy mistakes</string>
    <string name="mistake_1">No diagonals. Straight across only.</string>
    <string name="mistake_2">You can\'t hop over an empty hole. There has to be a peg in between.</string>
    <string name="mistake_3">Don\'t chase pegs into the corners. Nobody can hop a corner peg later.</string>
    <string name="rules_source">First printed in France in 1697 (Mercure galant). Hops by the same peg in a row count as one move; 18 moves is the fewest for a full solve (Bergholt, 1912).</string>
    <string name="history_title">Where it comes from</string>
    <string name="history_show">Show</string>
    <string name="history_hide">Hide</string>
    <string name="history_1">First printed in 1697 in the French magazine Mercure galant; the same year a portrait of the Princess of Soubise shows her with a board.</string>
    <string name="history_2">Two boards exist: English 33-hole (this one) and European 37-hole.</string>
    <string name="history_3">The Chinese names \"Kongming chess\" and \"solo diamond\" are modern; nothing to do with Zhuge Liang.</string>
    <string name="history_4">Bergholt found an 18-move solution in 1912; Beasley proved in 1964 that none is shorter.</string>
